5.4  Computing on the GPU, and when your program can't see it

TL;DR
  - Compute frameworks talk to the GPU through a vendor stack: CUDA for NVIDIA,
    ROCm (HIP) for AMD, oneAPI for Intel. Vulkan and OpenCL work across vendors.
  - Each needs the kernel driver, the matching user-space libraries, and access
    to device files: /dev/nvidia*, or /dev/kfd and /dev/dri/renderD*.
  - When a program says "no GPU", check those three, in that order.

      nvidia-smi                  # NVIDIA: driver, CUDA version, memory, users
      rocminfo | grep -i name     # AMD
      ls -l /dev/dri/; groups     # render nodes; are you in their group?

On your machine
  card0     Intel, driver i915, shows the boot screen
  Screens   eDP-1 1920x1200 · free: DP-1, HDMI-A-1
  Compute   /dev/dri/renderD128

Pitfalls
  - Render nodes usually belong to the render group (sometimes video): add
    yourself, then log in again (15.3).
  - Containers see no GPU unless given one: --gpus all with the NVIDIA Container
    Toolkit, or --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri for ROCm.
  - A Python package built for CPU only, or for another CUDA version, reports no
    GPU though nvidia-smi works: install the build that matches.
  - CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES or HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES set to an empty or wrong value
    hides GPUs from a program.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 5.4 --why

Related
  Commands  nvidia-smi, rocminfo, clinfo, groups
  Paths     /dev/dri/, /dev/kfd, /dev/nvidia0
  See also  idk 5.1 What a GPU does differently
  See also  idk 5.2 GPU drivers: the kernel, Mesa and NVIDIA
  See also  idk 15.3 sudo and groups
